      "n_test": 6379,
      "n_classes_evaluated": 29,
      "classes_missing_from_train": []
    },
    "interpretation": "Unlike the pre-Kaggle-merge dataset (where burst-disjoint splitting collapsed accuracy to 0% because almost every class had exactly one burst), burst-disjoint and naive-random splits now give SIMILAR results. This is not because leakage was fixed -- it's because burst_id is near-unique per row for 97% of the current data (Kaggle-sourced), so grouping by burst barely constrains the split at all. burst_id is not a meaningful leakage control at the current dataset composition."
  },
